        "content": "## Problem Statement\n**Exercise 1**: Given the function $f(x) = x^2 + 2x + 1$, find:\n\n1. The *derivative* $f'(x)$\n2. The **integral** $\\int f(x) dx$\n\n### Solution\nThe derivative is:\n$$f'(x) = 2x + 2$$\n\nThe integral is:\n$$\\int f(x) dx = \\frac{x^3}{3} + x^2 + x + C$$\n\n## Linear Algebra with Lists\nProperties of the matrix $A = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}$:\n\n- Determinant: $\\det(A) = ad - bc$\n- Trace: $\\text{tr}(A) = a + d$\n- Inverse (if exists):\n  $$A^{-1} = \\frac{1}{ad - bc}\\begin{pmatrix} d & -b \\\\ -c & a \\end{pmatrix}$$\n\n## Real World Context\n> **Note**: In economics, the cost function $C(q) = 100 + 5q$ means fixed costs are \\$100 and variable costs are \\$5 per unit.\n\nMoney context: If you buy 10 units, that's \\$100 fixed plus \\$50 variable = \\$150 total.\n",
